    "result_text": "The build succeeds \u2014 the test suite (the acceptance test being the only test class in the project) passes green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule \"reject a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date; respond with 400\" following the existing Spring conventions in the codebase, keeping each piece of logic in its own single-responsibility unit and mirroring the established validator/exception/advice pattern:\n\n1. **`RegistrationDateValidator`** (new, in `rest/validation/`) \u2014 a small `@Component` that throws when the supplied date is after `LocalDate.now()`. It skips `null` (the date is optional and defaults server-side), matching how `DailyOwnerLimitValidator` treats an absent date.\n\n2. **`FutureRegistrationDateException`** (new, in `rest/error/`) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying the rejected date, modeled on `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException`.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 injected the new validator (constructor + field, alphabetically consistent with siblings) and invoked it in the validation chain, reusing the DTO's `getRegistrationDate()` already passed to the daily-limit validator.\n\n4. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` returning `400 Bad Request` with an `errors: [\"registrationDate\"]` property, consistent with the other field-validation handlers (telephone/email/postcode).\n\nNo existing code was duplicated \u2014 the controller reuses the same DTO accessor and the advice reuses the shared `detailBuild` helper and `ERROR_INVALID_REQUEST` constant.",
